WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.455 --> 00:00:01.881
- What is your purpose?

00:00:01.881 --> 00:00:03.669
What is your mission with this business?

00:00:03.669 --> 00:00:05.934
You have to be unafraid of the fact

00:00:05.934 --> 00:00:08.342
that you will fail from time to time.

00:00:08.342 --> 00:00:09.872
If you have a knack for something

00:00:09.872 --> 00:00:11.687
and you have a passion for it,

00:00:11.687 --> 00:00:13.478
that makes life a lot easier.

00:00:13.478 --> 00:00:14.529
You may not love your neighbor,

00:00:14.529 --> 00:00:16.566
but you sure want to do
business with your neighbor.

00:00:16.566 --> 00:00:18.348
What makes a successful leader?

00:00:18.348 --> 00:00:20.853
Persuading others, sharing a vision,

00:00:20.853 --> 00:00:23.671
sharing hardship, earning trust.

00:00:23.671 --> 00:00:24.504
- Hello, Believe Nation.

00:00:24.504 --> 00:00:25.437
I'm Evan Carmichael.

00:00:25.437 --> 00:00:26.880
My one word is believe,

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:29.131
and I believe that
entrepreneurs are going to solve

00:00:29.131 --> 00:00:31.390
all of the world's major problems.

00:00:31.390 --> 00:00:32.738
So to help you on your journey,

00:00:32.738 --> 00:00:34.952
today we're going to learn
from Forbes Editor-in-Chief

00:00:34.952 --> 00:00:38.862
Steve Forbes and my take on
his top ten rules for success.

00:00:38.862 --> 00:00:40.429
Rule number one is my personal favorite,

00:00:40.429 --> 00:00:42.650
and I'd love to know which
one you guys like the best.

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:44.434
And as always, as you're listening,

00:00:44.434 --> 00:00:46.050
if something really resonates with you,

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:48.165
if there's a message that
really holds true to you,

00:00:48.165 --> 00:00:49.828
please leave it down in the comments below

00:00:49.828 --> 00:00:52.856
and put it in quotes so that
other people can be inspired,

00:00:52.856 --> 00:00:54.221
and when you write it down,

00:00:54.221 --> 00:00:57.316
it's much more likely to
stick with yourself as well.

00:00:57.316 --> 00:00:58.149
Enjoy.

00:01:01.482 --> 00:01:05.315
(dramatic instrumental music)

00:01:11.332 --> 00:01:12.679
- What is your purpose?

00:01:12.679 --> 00:01:15.041
What is your mission with this business?

00:01:15.041 --> 00:01:18.929
And if you have purpose,
then money will come

00:01:18.929 --> 00:01:21.234
if you achieve that purpose.

00:01:21.234 --> 00:01:23.961
And so if you're good
at a certain business,

00:01:23.961 --> 00:01:26.613
good at a certain product or service,

00:01:26.613 --> 00:01:27.966
that's your purpose in life.

00:01:27.966 --> 00:01:30.241
Go pursue it and you'll do a lot more good

00:01:30.241 --> 00:01:32.485
than doing something
where you don't have that

00:01:32.485 --> 00:01:34.106
particular ability.

00:01:34.106 --> 00:01:37.378
Free markets, free markets is about people

00:01:37.378 --> 00:01:40.348
and trying to do things with each other.

00:01:40.348 --> 00:01:44.515
And if you don't try to
use the power of the state

00:01:46.433 --> 00:01:49.541
to achieve success, then you succeed.

00:01:49.541 --> 00:01:51.667
You know, the reason
free markets are moral

00:01:51.667 --> 00:01:54.583
is because you succeed
by meeting the needs

00:01:54.583 --> 00:01:56.741
and wants of other people.

00:01:56.741 --> 00:01:59.190
If you don't, you don't succeed.

00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:01.994
So even if you're that
Hollywood caricature

00:02:01.994 --> 00:02:06.161
of just loving money and the
kind of nasty personality

00:02:06.161 --> 00:02:08.577
that makes babies cry and dogs bark at you

00:02:08.577 --> 00:02:11.123
when you go down the street,

00:02:11.123 --> 00:02:13.892
you're not going to get money, wealth,

00:02:13.892 --> 00:02:16.695
unless you provide something
somebody else wants.

00:02:16.695 --> 00:02:18.139
What's the difference between us

00:02:18.139 --> 00:02:20.351
and people of the Stone Age?

00:02:20.351 --> 00:02:23.934
Basic difference is we
have more knowledge.

00:02:24.904 --> 00:02:26.874
That's the basic difference.

00:02:26.874 --> 00:02:29.507
Even if you suffer physical catastrophe,

00:02:29.507 --> 00:02:31.355
tsunamis, wars,

00:02:31.355 --> 00:02:32.959
if knowledge is not destroyed,

00:02:32.959 --> 00:02:34.525
you can come back again,

00:02:34.525 --> 00:02:36.377
rebuild, and move forward.

00:02:36.377 --> 00:02:39.296
And knowledge comes from
constant experimentation,

00:02:39.296 --> 00:02:42.258
what Thomas Edison called trial and error.

00:02:42.258 --> 00:02:43.541
And that's why out in the world,

00:02:43.541 --> 00:02:45.727
you have to be unafraid of the fact

00:02:45.727 --> 00:02:48.548
that you will fail from time to time.

00:02:48.548 --> 00:02:51.166
It's never a smooth path.

00:02:51.166 --> 00:02:52.139
And you'll be jostled.

00:02:52.139 --> 00:02:54.626
You'll get crisis you didn't anticipate.

00:02:54.626 --> 00:02:56.457
It's part of learning.

00:02:56.457 --> 00:03:01.454
And also failure does not mean
nothing good comes from it.

00:03:01.454 --> 00:03:02.607
None of you are old enough,

00:03:02.607 --> 00:03:04.840
but back in the '80s and '90s,

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:06.745
Apple came out with a new product,

00:03:06.745 --> 00:03:09.266
late '80s, early 1990s,

00:03:09.266 --> 00:03:10.401
called the Newton.

00:03:10.401 --> 00:03:11.714
It was a handheld device.

00:03:11.714 --> 00:03:14.059
It flopped in the marketplace.

00:03:14.059 --> 00:03:16.983
But the technology in that failure

00:03:16.983 --> 00:03:20.017
laid the basis for Steve
Jobs in the future,

00:03:20.017 --> 00:03:22.267
for the iPad, iPhone, iPod.

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:27.731
What we might call it noble failure,

00:03:27.731 --> 00:03:29.343
learning from what doesn't work,

00:03:29.343 --> 00:03:31.530
experimentation, searching.

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:34.542
If you are an entrepreneur
who's done something,

00:03:34.542 --> 00:03:36.593
well, money means you've provided

00:03:36.593 --> 00:03:39.606
a product or service
that other people want.

00:03:39.606 --> 00:03:42.329
So you're getting resources in return.

00:03:42.329 --> 00:03:45.547
And it's not a matter of having
more than three meals a day

00:03:45.547 --> 00:03:48.130
or five meals or whatever satisfies you.

00:03:48.130 --> 00:03:48.963
- Right.
- It's how you

00:03:48.963 --> 00:03:51.376
reinvest those resources.

00:03:51.376 --> 00:03:53.168
Like Warren Buffett, long ago,

00:03:53.168 --> 00:03:54.769
he had more than he could ever use

00:03:54.769 --> 00:03:56.917
in terms of a material sense.

00:03:56.917 --> 00:04:01.039
But by reinvesting smartly,
increasing capital,

00:04:01.039 --> 00:04:04.313
he made it possible for pension
funds to increase in value,

00:04:04.313 --> 00:04:06.360
shareholders to have increased value,

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:08.513
companies to be able to expand.

00:04:08.513 --> 00:04:11.322
How do a lot of people get rich?

00:04:11.322 --> 00:04:14.976
In Hollywood movies, you do
it by killing your customers,

00:04:14.976 --> 00:04:17.554
polluting water and
all that kind of thing.

00:04:17.554 --> 00:04:19.353
But in the real world,

00:04:19.353 --> 00:04:22.165
it's often having faith in the future,

00:04:22.165 --> 00:04:25.079
putting together teams who work together,

00:04:25.079 --> 00:04:26.500
and you take it,

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:29.240
you're the last one that gets rewarded.

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:31.803
You got to pay your
workers, pay your suppliers,

00:04:31.803 --> 00:04:35.448
keep your creditors happy,
your financiers happy,

00:04:35.448 --> 00:04:38.645
and then if it works,
then you get something.

00:04:38.645 --> 00:04:41.861
But otherwise, you're last in line.

00:04:41.861 --> 00:04:43.444
And one of the things about,

00:04:43.444 --> 00:04:45.977
we discuss in the book about free markets

00:04:45.977 --> 00:04:48.980
is contrary to Hollywood imagery,

00:04:48.980 --> 00:04:50.303
most novelist's imagery,

00:04:50.303 --> 00:04:53.491
including occasionally
F. Scott Fitzgerald,

00:04:53.491 --> 00:04:56.772
who had something of a
chip on the shoulder,

00:04:56.772 --> 00:04:59.522
is that in terms of free markets,

00:05:00.413 --> 00:05:03.340
it does develop faith in the future.

00:05:03.340 --> 00:05:06.430
If you didn't have faith in the future,

00:05:06.430 --> 00:05:09.300
you would just be living for the moment.

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:11.072
And so it does bring about

00:05:11.072 --> 00:05:13.439
and it reduces barriers to humanity.

00:05:13.439 --> 00:05:16.396
What is it you are trying to do?

00:05:16.396 --> 00:05:18.576
And if you remind yourself of what is it

00:05:18.576 --> 00:05:20.307
you're trying to do,

00:05:20.307 --> 00:05:21.606
especially in the business world

00:05:21.606 --> 00:05:24.495
where things are so changing so rapidly,

00:05:24.495 --> 00:05:26.434
if you remind yourself
of what your goal is,

00:05:26.434 --> 00:05:29.059
then you don't get quite
as upset or disoriented

00:05:29.059 --> 00:05:33.036
when the means or the tools
to achieve that goal change.

00:05:33.036 --> 00:05:36.519
Well, everyone has knack for something.

00:05:36.519 --> 00:05:37.638
Doesn't mean you're going to,

00:05:37.638 --> 00:05:38.471
if you love tennis,

00:05:38.471 --> 00:05:40.412
doesn't mean you're
going to go to Wimbledon,

00:05:40.412 --> 00:05:43.477
or you have a knack for software writing

00:05:43.477 --> 00:05:45.861
you're going to become
Bill Gates or something.

00:05:45.861 --> 00:05:47.785
But if you have a knack for something

00:05:47.785 --> 00:05:49.612
and you have a passion for it,

00:05:49.612 --> 00:05:51.460
that makes life a lot easier,

00:05:51.460 --> 00:05:53.557
where a job actually doesn't just mean

00:05:53.557 --> 00:05:55.288
meeting the needs of life,

00:05:55.288 --> 00:05:58.023
but something you look
forward to each day,

00:05:58.023 --> 00:05:59.877
something where you
feel you have a purpose,

00:05:59.877 --> 00:06:02.084
you're doing something well.

00:06:02.084 --> 00:06:04.584
That gives you the intangibles

00:06:06.009 --> 00:06:09.530
that makes life just more than
looking for the next weekend.

00:06:09.530 --> 00:06:10.517
You may not love your neighbor,

00:06:10.517 --> 00:06:13.458
but you sure want to do
business with your neighbor.

00:06:13.458 --> 00:06:14.908
And we take it for granted,

00:06:14.908 --> 00:06:16.644
not to digress too much from the question,

00:06:16.644 --> 00:06:19.390
but we take it for granted in this country

00:06:19.390 --> 00:06:20.746
that if you start something,

00:06:20.746 --> 00:06:23.377
you want to get the best people possible.

00:06:23.377 --> 00:06:26.615
You get as much talent as you can.

00:06:26.615 --> 00:06:30.097
And that's fairly new in human affairs.

00:06:30.097 --> 00:06:31.722
Wasn't till very recent times

00:06:31.722 --> 00:06:34.778
you trust anyone beyond
your immediate family

00:06:34.778 --> 00:06:38.251
or your village or ethnic group.

00:06:38.251 --> 00:06:40.072
Just couldn't do it.

00:06:40.072 --> 00:06:43.379
But commerce does break
down those barriers

00:06:43.379 --> 00:06:46.757
and have people interact in ways

00:06:46.757 --> 00:06:49.522
that wouldn't have happened otherwise.

00:06:49.522 --> 00:06:53.706
And we see it in the global
economy we have today.

00:06:53.706 --> 00:06:56.439
Hugely complex supply chains.

00:06:56.439 --> 00:06:59.405
- How did you develop your understanding

00:06:59.405 --> 00:07:02.516
of economics and economic policy?

00:07:02.516 --> 00:07:04.631
- Just a fascination with it.

00:07:04.631 --> 00:07:07.589
Economics is people, markets, people.

00:07:07.589 --> 00:07:09.627
When they talk about impersonal markets,

00:07:09.627 --> 00:07:11.339
that's usually code for you don't like

00:07:11.339 --> 00:07:12.915
what people are doing.

00:07:12.915 --> 00:07:15.498
And so how do you have systems,

00:07:16.813 --> 00:07:18.357
going back to our founders,

00:07:18.357 --> 00:07:20.878
Alexander Hamilton, Ben
Franklin and others.

00:07:20.878 --> 00:07:24.519
We all have innate ambitions, energies,

00:07:24.519 --> 00:07:26.626
some good, some not so good.

00:07:26.626 --> 00:07:28.505
So how do you channel
these innate energies

00:07:28.505 --> 00:07:31.315
in a way that is constructive,

00:07:31.315 --> 00:07:32.815
that is not zero-sum?

00:07:32.815 --> 00:07:34.631
That's what commerce is all about.

00:07:34.631 --> 00:07:37.055
Your success is other people's success.

00:07:37.055 --> 00:07:39.174
In terms of leadership itself,

00:07:39.174 --> 00:07:41.566
while times and circumstances change,

00:07:41.566 --> 00:07:44.225
human nature does not change.

00:07:44.225 --> 00:07:46.189
The essence of leadership,

00:07:46.189 --> 00:07:49.557
the characteristics that
make for success and failure

00:07:49.557 --> 00:07:52.412
or success leading you into failure,

00:07:52.412 --> 00:07:55.112
those characteristics do not change.

00:07:55.112 --> 00:07:57.131
What makes a successful leader?

00:07:57.131 --> 00:07:59.381
Persuading others, sharing a vision,

00:07:59.381 --> 00:08:02.101
sharing hardship, earning trust.

00:08:02.101 --> 00:08:03.746
Those things remain the same.

00:08:03.746 --> 00:08:07.085
My one lesson I'll give you
today on the facts about today.

00:08:07.085 --> 00:08:08.350
The world, especially now,

00:08:08.350 --> 00:08:10.393
needs a global currency.

00:08:10.393 --> 00:08:13.389
Stability, standard measure of value.

00:08:13.389 --> 00:08:15.535
If you ever find yourself in an airplane,

00:08:15.535 --> 00:08:17.328
you want a little bit of elbow room,

00:08:17.328 --> 00:08:20.926
start talking to your seatmates
about monetary policy.

00:08:20.926 --> 00:08:23.759
You'll have all the room you want.

00:08:25.643 --> 00:08:26.979
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:08:26.979 --> 00:08:30.636
I made this video because
Arthur Robinson Jr. asked me to.

00:08:30.636 --> 00:08:31.937
If there's someone
you'd like me to profile

00:08:31.937 --> 00:08:33.478
in the next top 10,

00:08:33.478 --> 00:08:35.075
check out the link in the description

00:08:35.075 --> 00:08:37.212
and go and cast your vote.

00:08:37.212 --> 00:08:38.522
I'd also love to know what was

00:08:38.522 --> 00:08:40.090
your favorite message from this video.

00:08:40.090 --> 00:08:41.462
What did you learn from this video

00:08:41.462 --> 00:08:43.480
that you're going to immediately apply

00:08:43.480 --> 00:08:45.546
somehow in your life or in your business?

00:08:45.546 --> 00:08:47.117
Please leave it down
in the comments below.

00:08:47.117 --> 00:08:48.245
I'm really curious to find out.

00:08:48.245 --> 00:08:50.226
I also want to give a quick
shout-out to Bo Hawkins.

00:08:50.226 --> 00:08:52.587
Bo, thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:08:52.587 --> 00:08:55.846
Your One Word, and posting
the review on Twitter.

00:08:55.846 --> 00:08:57.894
I really, really, really
appreciate the support, man,

00:08:57.894 --> 00:08:59.898
and I'm so glad that you enjoyed the book.

00:08:59.898 --> 00:09:01.038
So thank you guys again for watching.

00:09:01.038 --> 00:09:02.262
I believe in you.

00:09:02.262 --> 00:09:04.279
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:09:04.279 --> 00:09:06.627
and whatever your one word is.

00:09:06.627 --> 00:09:08.053
Much love.

00:09:08.053 --> 00:09:09.553
I'll see you soon.

00:09:15.074 --> 00:09:17.685
- Take for example
cellphones or handhelds,

00:09:17.685 --> 00:09:19.422
whatever we call 'em today.

00:09:19.422 --> 00:09:21.012
So the first one 30 years ago,

00:09:21.012 --> 00:09:22.798
big as a shoebox.

00:09:22.798 --> 00:09:24.902
(audience laughing)

00:09:24.902 --> 00:09:26.402
Battery life 40 minutes.

00:09:26.402 --> 00:09:28.997
(audience laughing)

00:09:28.997 --> 00:09:32.164
The first one out of Motorola, $3,995.

00:09:35.190 --> 00:09:36.680
Today, they're everywhere.

00:09:36.680 --> 00:09:38.967
They do everything except maybe grow hair.

00:09:38.967 --> 00:09:41.496
Maybe they'll do that on the next round.

00:09:41.496 --> 00:09:42.329
It's amazing.

00:09:42.329 --> 00:09:44.017
Everyone can afford it.

00:09:44.017 --> 00:09:46.071
Seven billion around the world.

00:09:46.071 --> 00:09:47.926
The whole world is now at your fingertips.

00:09:47.926 --> 00:09:50.762
We're so spoiled that if we call Mongolia

00:09:50.762 --> 00:09:52.306
and it takes more than five seconds,

00:09:52.306 --> 00:09:54.214
we say what a lousy service business.

00:09:54.214 --> 00:09:57.129
(audience laughing)

00:09:57.129 --> 00:09:57.962
It's amazing.

00:09:57.962 --> 00:09:59.230
It's amazing.

00:09:59.230 --> 00:10:02.085
But these kind of miracles
become commonplace

00:10:02.085 --> 00:10:04.744
when entrepreneurs are
allowed to do great things.

00:10:04.744 --> 00:10:06.315
We all benefit from it.

00:10:06.315 --> 00:10:07.977
What Lincoln talked about,

00:10:07.977 --> 00:10:10.690
the opportunity to
improve our lot in life,

00:10:10.690 --> 00:10:12.340
that's what makes this country great.

00:10:12.340 --> 00:10:13.422
It doesn't matter where you're from,

00:10:13.422 --> 00:10:14.636
what you've done before.

00:10:14.636 --> 00:10:16.548
You can do great things.

00:10:16.548 --> 00:10:17.784
- If the message is true,

00:10:17.784 --> 00:10:19.435
if we can really have our wishes,

00:10:19.435 --> 00:10:21.469
if dreams do come true,

00:10:21.469 --> 00:10:23.207
and theirs certainly isn't.

00:10:23.207 --> 00:10:25.627
They get up every day and go
do something they don't enjoy

00:10:25.627 --> 00:10:26.838
with people they don't respect

00:10:26.838 --> 00:10:29.156
for money that's not sufficient.

00:10:29.156 --> 00:10:30.431
What would be their first step

00:10:30.431 --> 00:10:33.321
toward going from where they
are to where they want to be?

00:10:33.321 --> 00:10:34.922
- Well, if they have the opportunity

00:10:34.922 --> 00:10:37.586
is to step back and say okay,

00:10:37.586 --> 00:10:39.464
if I had a blank sheet of paper,

00:10:39.464 --> 00:10:41.442
what would I be doing each day?

00:10:41.442 --> 00:10:42.417
And don't say nothing,

00:10:42.417 --> 00:10:44.616
'cause that's the
hardest job in the world.

00:10:44.616 --> 00:10:46.948
Figure out what you have a passion for

00:10:46.948 --> 00:10:49.671
and being willing to apply the discipline

00:10:49.671 --> 00:10:51.090
to achieve that passion.

00:10:51.090 --> 00:10:53.320
When we go to a sporting
match, for example,

00:10:53.320 --> 00:10:55.915
we see the end result of people,

00:10:55.915 --> 00:10:57.935
many people who've done a lot of sweat,

00:10:57.935 --> 00:10:59.400
a lot of training,

00:10:59.400 --> 00:11:02.822
a lot of passion to
achieve their abilities,

00:11:02.822 --> 00:11:06.064
but we don't see all the work
that goes behind the scenes.

00:11:06.064 --> 00:11:08.418
So if you're not prepared
to deal with the grunt work,

00:11:08.418 --> 00:11:10.677
the scut work, the discipline,

00:11:10.677 --> 00:11:12.021
and that's why it's often said

00:11:12.021 --> 00:11:16.395
people who work hard in
terms of a disciplined way

00:11:16.395 --> 00:11:18.465
end up being freer than those

00:11:18.465 --> 00:11:20.115
who don't have to do anything,

00:11:20.115 --> 00:11:22.174
because then they can't do anything.

00:11:22.174 --> 00:11:23.751
A number of years ago,

00:11:23.751 --> 00:11:26.296
a fellow named George Mitchell in Texas

00:11:26.296 --> 00:11:29.003
had a natural gas company, not a big one,

00:11:29.003 --> 00:11:31.296
and he noticed something
that everyone else noticed,

00:11:31.296 --> 00:11:34.347
and that is this country is
running out of natural gas.

00:11:34.347 --> 00:11:35.584
So he asked his engineers,

00:11:35.584 --> 00:11:38.871
can't we find a way to
get it out of shale?

00:11:38.871 --> 00:11:40.192
We have a lot of shale,

00:11:40.192 --> 00:11:42.238
tons of shale around the world,

00:11:42.238 --> 00:11:43.857
so can we get it out?

00:11:43.857 --> 00:11:45.410
And the engineers said no way.

00:11:45.410 --> 00:11:47.220
Just not economical.

00:11:47.220 --> 00:11:48.309
And he pushed it.

00:11:48.309 --> 00:11:51.845
He put his own money into research,

00:11:51.845 --> 00:11:54.621
and ultimately, the breakthrough came,

00:11:54.621 --> 00:11:57.254
and now we're drowning in natural gas.

00:11:57.254 --> 00:11:59.705
Natural gas is $2 or $3.

00:11:59.705 --> 00:12:04.622
In Europe, it's $12, $13 for
about a million thermal units.

00:12:04.622 --> 00:12:07.145
And the amazing thing is, thousand keep,

00:12:07.145 --> 00:12:08.951
the amazing thing is,

00:12:08.951 --> 00:12:12.329
it's not only means we're going
to have cheap energy again.

00:12:12.329 --> 00:12:14.085
Not only in a few years we will be

00:12:14.085 --> 00:12:16.209
exporting energy in a major way.

00:12:16.209 --> 00:12:19.070
The government doesn't stop it.

00:12:19.070 --> 00:12:21.196
Not only does that mean
we're not going to have,

00:12:21.196 --> 00:12:22.888
we're going to have less diesel,

00:12:22.888 --> 00:12:24.902
which is not great for the environment,

00:12:24.902 --> 00:12:28.575
we'll be able to do trucks on natural gas,

00:12:28.575 --> 00:12:31.565
but also change the
geopolitics in the world.

00:12:31.565 --> 00:12:33.288
One of the biggest natural gas producers

00:12:33.288 --> 00:12:35.748
up till now has been Russia.

00:12:35.748 --> 00:12:37.572
They have Western Europe, as they say,

00:12:37.572 --> 00:12:39.097
by the short hairs,

00:12:39.097 --> 00:12:42.416
because Western Europe's
dependent on natural gas.

00:12:42.416 --> 00:12:44.723
Big chunk of it comes from Russia.

00:12:44.723 --> 00:12:48.338
Well, if natural gas is
easy to get and cheap,

00:12:48.338 --> 00:12:51.253
then there goes Russia's leverage.

00:12:51.253 --> 00:12:53.628
There goes the Middle East's leverage,

00:12:53.628 --> 00:12:55.641
because this energy's everywhere

00:12:55.641 --> 00:12:58.355
if you're allowed to find it.

00:12:58.355 --> 00:13:02.741
So one entrepreneur whose
name nobody knows today

00:13:02.741 --> 00:13:05.270
outside of the energy industry

00:13:05.270 --> 00:13:07.404
is profoundly changing lives.

00:13:07.404 --> 00:13:10.317
This year, because of the fracking,

00:13:10.317 --> 00:13:14.484
American households are going
to spend $12 billion less

00:13:15.333 --> 00:13:18.098
on natural gas to heat
their homes this winter

00:13:18.098 --> 00:13:20.708
than they would've if
Mitchell hadn't invented,

00:13:20.708 --> 00:13:22.440
come up with, he and his engineers

00:13:22.440 --> 00:13:24.216
come up with fracking.

00:13:24.216 --> 00:13:26.446
So, and it's just beginning.

00:13:26.446 --> 00:13:28.196
It's just beginning.

